# Licensing

Copyright (C) 2026 Minice — https://minice.ai
Written and maintained by Mihailin (`mihailinl`) and the AstraPlugins
contributors.

AstraPlugins is the plugin SDK, CLI and specification set for **Astra**, a
Minice product.

This repository is **not** under a single licence. The line is drawn where it
changes what somebody else may build: code that ends up inside another
program is weak copyleft, and code that stands alone is strong copyleft.

| Path | Licence | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `astra-plugin-sdk/` | **MPL-2.0** | ends up inside every Rust plugin |
| `astra-plugin-sdk-python/` | **MPL-2.0** | imported by every Python plugin |
| `astra-plugin-sdk-ts/` | **MPL-2.0** | imported by every TypeScript plugin |
| `astra-plugin-macros/` | **MPL-2.0** | expands into the author's own source |
| `astra-plugin-cli/` (except `vendor/`) | **GPL-3.0-or-later** | a standalone tool; nothing links it |
| `astra-plugin-cli/vendor/astra-plugin-manifest/` | **MPL-2.0** | a shared library |
| `examples/` | **MPL-2.0** | starting points people copy |
| everything else (`docs/`, `spec/`, `tools/`, `proto/`, `testdata/`) | **GPL-3.0-or-later** | |

Full texts in [`LICENSES/`](LICENSES/): [MPL-2.0](LICENSES/MPL-2.0.txt) and
[GPL-3.0](LICENSES/GPL-3.0.txt), reproduced verbatim from mozilla.org and
gnu.org. Neither is modified — a modified licence is a different and
incompatible licence.

## What this means in practice

**Writing a plugin.** Your plugin is yours, under any licence you choose,
including a proprietary one. MPL is file-level copyleft: it reaches the files
it covers and nothing else, and it was written to be combined with code under
other terms — including statically, which is why it is here rather than the
LGPL, whose relinking requirement lands on exactly the people this is meant not
to burden.

*Being allowed to ship a proprietary plugin is not the same as being allowed to
list one.* The registry accepts open-source licences only; see its `POLICY.md`.
That is policy, revisable, and deliberately separate from this licence, which
is not.

**Modifying an SDK file.** Publish that file under MPL-2.0. You need not open
anything else — not your plugin, not your product, not your other files.

**Forking the CLI, the tooling, the specs or the docs.** GPL-3.0-or-later:
publish your changes under the same terms. Nothing links these, so nothing is
conceded. Taking this architecture, closing it and shipping it is what copyleft
exists to prevent.

**Attribution.** See [`NOTICE`](NOTICE). The GPL-licensed parts carry a
section 7(b) attribution requirement; the MPL-licensed parts require only that
the notices in the source files stay there, because MPL has no mechanism for
more and pretending otherwise would not be MPL.

## History

Before this commit the repository declared MIT in its package manifests and
carried no licence text at all. Anything obtained under those earlier terms
stays under them — a licence already granted cannot be withdrawn. Everything
from this commit onward is under the terms above.
